<p>Rising fuel costs, linked in part to the Iran war, are reshaping Easter and Holy Week travel across parts of the Asia-Pacific, though the effects vary sharply by country. In Australia, the government urged people to keep their Easter plans while buying only the fuel they need, even as hundreds of mostly rural petrol stations lost power and some ran dry. Officials said people should still feel free to travel to see family, and a cut to the fuel excise, along with clearer government messaging on supply, appears to have helped limit cancellations at holiday parks and resorts. In the Philippines, the picture is tougher. Surging gasoline prices have pushed some people to cancel or scale back Holy Week trips, including the Catholic Visita Iglesia tradition of visiting seven churches. Together, the reports show how a global energy shock is reaching everyday life, affecting faith traditions, family reunions, and tourism, while travelers and businesses adapt by combining trips, cutting back on driving, or sticking with plans a little more cautiously.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/6xf8brA9fcT4Bt8UL5NzbWVr1gLxmPHdQrgU_VajJR32V5Ho6_leNE6UhcKVWKzWt8tELZ4kf_fheli_Ye9FJbbEk13gdgum4aCE89acV8Hg2abIEaSziTy5YBLX5Be2IRQ9vRWO80Pk0W-kj6QCgdtgKVuhe2DPpp7j55SxDCyMduDUTzujhesVagE' alt='Filipinos heading to the provinces for the Holy Week lining up at a bus terminal in Quezon City, Philippines, on Thursday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Bookings hold: NRMA, which owns about 50 holiday parks and resorts, said cancellations rose only slightly instead of reaching the higher levels it first feared.</li><li>Driving changes: An NRMA survey in March found 24% of drivers were combining trips to make fuel go further, while more than 40% were cutting their driving by 10% to 30%.</li><li>Busy corridors: Transport for NSW said Easter remains one of the busiest road-trip periods and expects extra vehicles on major routes to regional areas despite cost-of-living pressures.</li><li>Faith tradition: In the Philippines, higher gasoline prices disrupted Visita Iglesia, a Holy Week custom in which Catholics visit seven churches.</li></ul><blockquote>We encourage people to feel free to stick to your plans, go and see your family, go take a break - but get no more fuel than you need. - Chris Bowen</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Chris Bowen, Australian energy minister: Bowen urged Australians to continue with Easter travel while avoiding unnecessary fuel purchases, describing the weekend as important for faith and family. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/stick-easter-travel-australians-told-though-hundreds-petrol-stations-dry'>Reuters via Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Peter Khoury, NRMA: Khoury said cancellations did happen, but stayed below early fears as supply became clearer, the excise cut took effect, and motorists adjusted their behavior. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/excise-cut-softens-blow-for-easter-road-trippers/'>Michael West</a>)</li><li>Rob Austin, Transport for NSW: Austin said Easter road trips may look different this year, but authorities still expect heavy traffic on key regional corridors. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/excise-cut-softens-blow-for-easter-road-trippers/'>Michael West</a>)</li><li>Amanda Craft, Western Sydney University: Craft said fuel price spikes around long weekends reflect predictable demand cycles as well as behavioral responses like panic buying by motorists. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/excise-cut-softens-blow-for-easter-road-trippers/'>Michael West</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/stick-easter-travel-australians-told-though-hundreds-petrol-stations-dry'>Stick to Easter travel, Australians told, though hundreds of petrol stations dry</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/excise-cut-softens-blow-for-easter-road-trippers/'>Excise cut softens blow for Easter road trippers</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/03/world/asia/philippines-holy-week-oil-fuel-prices.html'>In the Philippines, Rising Fuel Prices Force Travelers to Stay Home During Holy Week</a> - nytimes.com</li></ul>